 "110": {
  "class_type": "PrimitiveStringMultiline",
  "inputs": {
   "value": "Photorealistic animal reference sheet: one wide image on a seamless plain light grey studio backdrop, showing the same single real animal from the reference image <image1> eight times, now standing up on all four legs, and nothing else.\n\nThe animal is the one in <image1>, with exactly the same species, age, face, coat colour and markings, but in a new pose: standing on all four legs. The brief below adds what the image does not show. None of the brief's words appear in the image and any panel list in it is ignored:\n"
  },
  "_meta": {
   "title": "Sheet layout and consistency rules"
  }
 },
 "111": {
  "class_type": "PrimitiveStringMultiline",
  "inputs": {
   "value": "\n\nEight views in two rows of four, on one continuous backdrop with no frames. Top row, left to right, four full-body views of the animal standing on all four legs: side profile facing right, three-quarter front view, front view, back view. Bottom row, left to right: a fifth full-body view, standing on all four legs and seen in three-quarter view from behind, then three different tight head close-ups: one from the front, one in three-quarter view and one in side profile, each about three times as large as the heads of the full-body views.\n\nIn all five full-body views the animal is on its feet with its belly off the floor, never sitting, lying down or standing upright, even if it sits or lies in <image1>, at exactly the same size and build. It looks exactly its stated age: a baby animal stays a small baby with baby proportions. Its species, age, face, coat colour and markings are exactly those of <image1>. Accessories are as the brief describes them, or as in <image1> where the brief says nothing, and identical in every view. The backdrop, pose, lighting and framing of <image1> are not copied.\n\nA real studio photograph, 85 mm lens, soft even light, everything in sharp focus, natural colours. No text, labels, numbers, borders, frames or watermark."
  },
  "_meta": {
   "title": "Realism and no-text rules"
  }
 },
